  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 338 PM EDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\n===== Tuesday - Thursday =====\n\nThe upper ridge remains in place to the west for the middle of the \nweek, with persistent NW flow across the Ohio Valley. Tuesday will \nfeature a sharper mid-level shortwave pivoting across the Great \nLakes and into the Northeast, and will influence a sfc low to follow \na similar track. A trailing frontal boundary will sweep through the \nOhio Valley, and will be the focus for additional showers and storms \non Tuesday, with the greatest severe risk just east of the forecast \narea. Despite a lower confidence in forecast temps for Tuesday, we \nshould still be able to achieve some strong heating combined with \nincreased humidity to support strong destabilization through the \nafternoon. PWATs near 2 inches are also expected, so heavy rain \nrates along with some stronger thunderstorms are possible in the \nafternoon. Steering flow should be slightly more by then, so storms \nlikely will be a bit more progressive to alleviate flooding concerns \nsome. Forecast temps have been knocked down from the NBM, but still \nhave upper 80s to lower 90s, combined with sfc dewpoints in the \nupper 70s, which could yield heat indices around 100F. There could \nbe a need for a Heat Advisory, but confidence in that remains very \nlow given the precip forecast which could derail our temp forecast. \n\nOur weather turns drier for the Wednesday-Thursday time frame as sfc \nhigh pressure settles across the upper Midwest. However, the upper \nridge to the west will begin to show signs of weakening by then, \nthough we'll still be in weak NW flow for the time being. Temps for \nboth days will be near normal in the upper 80s, with slightly lower \ndewpoints. \n\n\n===== Friday into the Weekend =====\n\nScattered showers and storms could make a return for Friday and into \nthe weekend as a compact upper low finally disrupts the upper ridge \nand moves across the central US. There is decent agreement between \nthe GFS/ECMWF for the upper low to come spinning over the Wabash \nValley over the weekend, which could keep us slightly more active \nover next weekend.",
